        "id": 29032,
        "name": "savagegreywolf",
        "username": "savagegreywolf",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/s/3ec8ea/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2012-04-26T16:59:20.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"&quot;&quot;broccoli&quot;&quot;\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n \"\"broccoli\"\":</div>\n<blockquote>\n<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"arenson9\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/48/1900_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> arenson9:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I disagree folks, though I admit that I’m not sure. I think it does 10 points of damage, not 18.</p>\n<p>Brain Burn reads:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The Visionary deals herself 1 Psychic damage for each card removed from the villain trash in this way.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Given that, as noted, there are other cards that say things like ‘a hero does X damage where X equals something or other’, I see the logic that since Brain Burn doesn’t use that same terminology, than Brain Burn is likely referring to separate attacks.</p>\n<p>As TheJayMann notes, however, there has been recognition that the wording of the cards is not always consistent, and there is precedent for the wording on Brain Burn to refer to a single attack. Consider, for instance, Haka of Battle:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Draw 2 cards, then discard 1 or more cards. Increase the next damage dealt by Haka by 1 for each card discarded this way.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is, admittedly, not a perfect example, as even if this refers to multiple separate increases, they would all still affect the next damage, so it is moot as to whether Haka of Battle refers to one increase or to many increases. Still, the similarity in wording between Brain Burn and Haka of Battle (or Haka of Restoration) suggests to me that the intent of Brain Burn was for there to be a single attack against Visionary.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I think the problem here is that you’re arguing intent vs. card text.  I’m fairly confident that as the card is written, the damage would be doubled.  As for designer intent… until we see errata, a correction via the enhanced edition, or comments from one of the &gt;G crew, it seems like playing it as written is the right choice.  At least, that’s my $0.02</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The problem is that as written it is ambiguous, as per my first post, when I read it I see it as a single instance of damage, and my brother read it as x individual instances of damage, where x is the number of cards.</p>\n<p>Another reason I feel it is a single instance of damage is that, as Visionary does it to herself, putting her in the same team as Legacy, his leadership skills… lead her to do more damage to herself?</p>\n<p>Particularly problematic here when the +damage is coming from buffs to your team, and Legacy can buff hero damage twice, which would make the 9 card situation do more damage to Visionary than her max HP, all due to a ‘buff’ from Legacy.</p>\n<p>I really think we need an official ruling regarding damage done by heroes to themselves and buffs to damage at least, as this has been a problem in some other situations as well (see: Fanatic).</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
